    // ── FAQ ──
    faq_h2: "Frequently asked questions",
    faq_q1: "What is a \"null model\" and what is it for?",
    faq_a1: "It's the <b>yardstick for randomness</b>. For each real event, we generate 200 fictional events on nearby dates (±30 days) at the same location. If Mercury-Uranus appears equally often in the real events as in the fictional ones, the result is zero. If it appears significantly more often, there's a clue. Without this step, any analysis would give misleading results — slow planets (Neptune, Pluto) remain in the same position for decades and would falsify the results.",
    faq_q2: "Why are 398 configurations tested simultaneously? Doesn't that increase false positives?",
    faq_a2: "Yes, which is why we apply the <span class=\"glos\" data-glos=\"FDR\">Benjamini-Hochberg FDR</span> correction. If you test 398 things at 5% confidence, you'd expect ~20 false positives by chance. The FDR correction controls that: it only reports as significant what exceeds the adjusted threshold. Additionally, candidates are tested in an independent sample (<span class=\"glos\" data-glos=\"split validation\">cross-validation</span>).",
    faq_q3: "Are astronomical data calculated automatically?",
    faq_a3: "Yes. You only provide the <b>date, time and location</b> of the event. The program uses the JPL DE421 ephemeris (NASA) to calculate the exact positions of 10 planets. No knowledge of astrology is needed to enter data.",
    faq_q4: "Can it be used for medical or social events, without precise lat/lon?",
    faq_a4: "Yes, with one limitation: without precise coordinates, <b>astrological houses</b> (which depend on location) cannot be calculated. But <b>planet–planet aspects</b> (like Mercury–Uranus) are the same everywhere on Earth at a given moment, so they are analyzable. Global domains (solar storms) already work this way.",
    faq_q5: "Is this astrology? What does science say?",
    faq_a5: "The methodology is <b>standard statistics applied to an unconventional subject</b>. The analysis doesn't \"believe\" in anything: it compares observed frequencies against expected frequencies by chance, using the same methods as epidemiology or genomics. If the result is null, it's reported as null. If there's an association, it's reported with all its limitations. The question of what causes the association, if it exists, is a further step.",
    faq_q6: "Why do you use terms like \"conjunction\" or \"zodiac sign\"? Isn't that astrology?",
    hip_hist_h2: "Historical hypotheses",
    hip_hist_p: "These hypotheses were qualitatively formulated by cosmobiology's founders. This platform allows them to be put to rigorous quantitative testing for the first time, with global data and a matched null model.",
    hip_h1_title: "H1 · Pluto in prominent configuration → earthquakes / volcanoes",
    hip_h1_p: "Raynaud de la Ferrière (<em>Grandes Mensajes</em>) claimed that Pluto \"is always in a powerful celestial configuration when violent earthquakes or volcanic eruptions occur.\" Feature to test: <code>aspecto|Pluton|*|conjuncion</code> or <code>angular|Pluton|ASC</code> in the GCMT M≥7 and GVP volcano catalogs.",
    hip_pill_explor: "Exploratory",
    hip_h1_nota: "Pre-register before running",
    hip_h2_title: "H2 · Lunar position at the moment of the earthquake (Llona / Cycloidal Theory)",
    hip_h2_p: "Llona (Cycloidal Theory) points out that lunar tidal forces modulate geodynamic activity. This hypothesis has two distinct readings:",
    hip_h2_li1: "<b>Temporal periodicity</b> (more earthquakes every ~29.5 days): <b>not confirmed</b> in declustered GCMT — serial correlation R<sub>h</sub>=−0.001, p=0.93. The clean catalog is compatible with a Poisson process with no lunar rhythm.",
    hip_h2_li2: "<b>Lunar position at the moment of the earthquake</b> (phase, distance, angularity): <b>on hold</b> — <code>lunar_distance|apogeo</code> (z=+4.44) and <code>angular|Moon|ASC</code> (z=+5.41) came from the catalog inflated 5.5× by the magnitude error; on the corrected one they give z=+2.25 and z=+0.82, below their detection floor. Not enough power to conclude, in either direction. This is more faithful to Llona: not \"more earthquakes every month,\" but \"more earthquakes when the Moon is in a certain position.\"",
    hip_h2_nota: "The positional version is the pre-registration candidate",
    hip_h3_title: "H3 · Moon at apogee → higher seismicity (already a candidate)",
    hip_h3_p: "Emerged from the declustered GCMT analysis with z=+4.44 (p_fdr &lt; 0.001), <b>but on the catalog with the magnitude error</b>; on the corrected one it gives z=+2.25 with a detection floor of RR≥1.19 — not enough power to decide. Consistent with Llona: at apogee the Moon's orbital speed changes such that the Earth tide varies — a possible window of lower \"damping\" of crustal stress. <b>Candidate for confirmatory pre-registration.</b>",
    hip_pill_candidata: "OSF candidate",
    fuentes_repo_h2: "📦 The project's repository",
    fuentes_repo_p: "All the code, catalogs, and this very page live in a public repository. You don't need to download anything to use the platform — this is for anyone who wants to reproduce an analysis, audit a result, or build a new module.",
