    mc_volver_movimientos: "↩ Back to movements",
    mc_llona_p2: "Note: this curve is a <b>separate</b> scene from the movements above (precession/nutation/galactic orbit) — but, unlike before, the Earth <b>really does rotate and revolve</b> when you press \"▶ Draw\": the green point marked at latitude φ travels stuck to the sphere, and its resulting path (seen from outside) is exactly the cycloidal curve being traced. Touching any checkbox or the slider above automatically returns you to the normal view.",
    mc_llona_q: "What's exact and what's schematic?",
    mc_llona_a: "Real: the rotation+revolution combination with the axis tilted ε≈23.44° DOES geometrically produce a cycloidal curve, not a circle — that's trigonometry, not a TCC interpretation. Schematic: here <b>Vt/Vr is a dimensionless ratio you choose</b> so you can SEE all three cases (the real Earth is always in the \"Normal\" case with Vt≈29.78 km/s ≫ Vr≈0.46 km/s at the equator — the \"Regressive\" case doesn't happen on the real Earth, it's shown only for comparison, as the script requests, with what would happen on bodies like Uranus). The \"Singular Points\" every 30° and the Rdr' curves (cones toward the Sun/Moon) are the graphic representation of Llona's hypothesis — there is no independent statistical test, within this platform, of whether those points coincide with real earthquakes (see <a href=\"#\" onclick=\"return irA('guia','')\">Methodology → Llona's Geodynamics</a> for what has actually been tested).",
