    mc_figuras_q: "Where do these figures come from and what does the extent mean?",
    mc_figuras_a: "Open data from the Stellarium project (asterisms by culture: which stars each tradition connects to draw its own Pisces/Aquarius). The extent shown is the ecliptic longitude spanned by that star FIGURE — not the administrative boundary (that's the one above, IAU/Delporte, ○ on the map). Only the modern Western one has both at once: traditional figure AND official boundary.",
    mc_medir_distancia: "📐 Measure angular distance",
    mc_medir_q: "How does it work?",
    mc_medir_a: "Equatorial coordinate system (Right Ascension / Declination), the same one the whole platform uses. Turn it on and click any two stars or planets — it measures the real angle between their two directions as seen from the origin (it doesn't depend on how \"far\" each one looks on this schematic map).",
    mc_leyenda_pasa_mouse: "Hover over any point to see its name, magnitude and real distance. Use the \"Go to\" selector to move around the map — choose a <b>planet</b> (Mercury to Pluto) to also see what sign it'll be in over the coming years.",
    mc_planeta_barras: "Each bar is the time spent in that sign — the color is its <b>element</b> (🔴 Fire · 🟤 Earth · 🟢 Air (aquamarine) · 🔵 Water). If the planet moves backward (retrograde) and returns to a previous sign for a few weeks, it appears as an extra segment — that's not an error, that's how the real sky looks.",
    mc_th_signo: "Sign",
    mc_th_elemento: "Element",
    mc_th_modalidad: "Modality",
    mc_th_desde: "From",
    mc_th_hasta: "To",
    mc_elemento_modalidad: "Element (Fire/Earth/Air/Water) and modality (Cardinal/Fixed/Mutable) are standard Western astrology categories, not measurements — the <i>sign and date</i> of each transit do come from the same Keplerian orbital model used throughout the Cosmic Map (± a few days of margin, not the precision of Event Capture with Skyfield/DE421).",
    mc_sol_planetas_mov: "🌀 <b>Sun + planets in motion</b> (real spiral, planets only, Sun only, does the galaxy spin?) are now buttons inside the map itself, next to ⏹ to stop them.",
    mc_espiral_q: "Why does it look like a spiral?",
    mc_espiral_a: "The planets orbit the Sun (faster the closer they are — Kepler's 3rd law) <b>and at the same time</b> the Sun, with the whole system, orbits the galactic center (much slower, oriented toward the real Galactic Center marked on the map). Seen together from outside, the real path of each planet isn't a closed circle: it's a spiral coiled around the Sun's trajectory — the 🌀 button traces that real path with a trail for Mercury, Earth and Jupiter. The galactic spin is still schematic/sped-up (real ≈230 million years, ≈220 km/s) — but the <i>ratio</i> between planetary speeds is real.",
    mc_gira_galaxia: "🌌 Does the galaxy spin?",
    mc_mueven_estrellas: "✨ Do the stars move?",
    mc_marco_referencia: "Reference frame:",
    mc_helio: "Heliocentric — as measured by the catalog",
    mc_anios_simulados: "Simulated years:",
    mc_lsr_q: "What is the \"LSR\" and why does it change what I see?",
    mc_lsr_a: "What a catalog measures is <b>apparent</b> motion, seen from the Sun — but the Sun also moves, so part of that motion is just the <b>reflection</b> of our own (like trees that seem to move backward when you're in a car). The <b>Local Standard of Rest</b> is the frame that follows the galactic rotation at the <i>average</i> speed of stars in the solar neighborhood. Subtracting the Sun's peculiar velocity relative to the LSR (U,V,W = 11.10, 12.24, 7.25 km/s — Schönrich, Binney &amp; Dehnen 2010; ≈18 km/s total toward the <a href=\"#\" onclick=\"threeIrAEstrella('→ Ápex solar');return false\">solar apex</a>) leaves each star's own proper motion, cleaner for studying the structure of the galactic disk. Honest note: the correction uses only proper motion and distance — without radial velocity the full 3D velocity of each star isn't reconstructed, but for motion across the sky this correction is indeed complete (the radial part doesn't produce proper motion).",
    mc_expanden_q: "Do they expand or contract?",
    mc_expanden_a: "Yes, they move — each one travels through space relative to the Sun at tens of km/s (\"proper motion\", Hipparcos catalog) — but from so far away it looks <b>tiny</b> (fractions of an arcsecond per year). They don't expand or contract: that's the expansion of the universe (Hubble), only relevant between very distant galaxies. The control above extrapolates thousands of years to show the real <i>direction</i> (straight line, an approximation — the real trajectory curves due to the galaxy's rotation).",
    mc_formulas_q: "📚 Where does each number come from? — formulas, catalogs and what others use",
    mc_formulas_intro: "None of this was invented for the map. Every figure has a citable source, and in several cases <b>more than one system is in use</b> giving different results — the differences are listed, not hidden.",
